WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:10.254
A café chain's card payments flow into Kafka, a program that keeps a log of messages for other programs to read.

2
00:00:10.254 --> 00:00:15.919
A counting program reads them, and counts the payments per minute for one shop, m-7.

3
00:00:16.167 --> 00:00:19.790
One payment a second, so every minute should say sixty.

4
00:00:19.790 --> 00:00:21.833
The question is when to say it.

5
00:00:21.833 --> 00:00:25.061
When is the minute from 0 to 60 seconds complete?

6
00:00:25.433 --> 00:00:28.151
The simple answer is the program's own clock.

7
00:00:28.151 --> 00:00:33.829
When it passes the minute's end, wait 10 more seconds for stragglers, then send out the count.

8
00:00:33.829 --> 00:00:37.635
A straggler is an event that arrives later than its neighbours.

9
00:00:37.867 --> 00:00:40.117
That works until the first redeploy.

10
00:00:40.117 --> 00:00:43.867
Stop the program for sixty seconds to install a new version.

11
00:00:43.867 --> 00:00:51.241
When it comes back, its clock says that minute is over, so the window closes before it reads a single waiting payment.

12
00:00:51.467 --> 00:00:56.833
The count goes out as zero, and the real payments arrive to find their window gone.

13
00:00:56.833 --> 00:00:59.096
A zero, sent with total confidence.

14
00:00:59.467 --> 00:01:01.472
The trouble is mixing two clocks.

15
00:01:01.472 --> 00:01:05.179
Event time is when the payment happened, stamped by the till.

16
00:01:05.179 --> 00:01:07.913
Processing time is when our program reads it.

17
00:01:08.167 --> 00:01:13.039
A friend on holiday sends you a postcard a day, and you want to add up July.

18
00:01:13.039 --> 00:01:15.604
The post is slow, and sometimes jumbled.

19
00:01:15.867 --> 00:01:17.591
So when do you add up July?

20
00:01:17.591 --> 00:01:19.826
Not when your calendar says August.

21
00:01:19.826 --> 00:01:25.956
When the latest postmark you've received passes the end of July, minus a few days for slow post.

22
00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:26.685
Map it.

23
00:01:26.685 --> 00:01:28.555
The postmark is event time.

24
00:01:28.555 --> 00:01:32.019
The latest postmark is how far the stream has got.

25
00:01:32.019 --> 00:01:35.276
The few days are your allowance for stragglers.

26
00:01:35.276 --> 00:01:38.116
And adding up July is closing the window.

27
00:01:38.333 --> 00:01:46.489
That latest postmark is called a watermark: the program's running claim, I believe no more events older than this will arrive.

28
00:01:46.767 --> 00:01:56.421
Kafka Streams, a library for writing such counting programs, uses the largest event time seen so far, and gives each window a grace period on top.

29
00:01:56.421 --> 00:02:03.100
When the watermark passes a window's end plus its grace, the window is final, and its count goes out.

30
00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:05.283
Sending it on is called emitting.

31
00:02:05.567 --> 00:02:08.250
Watch it with 10 seconds of grace.

32
00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:12.590
At 65 seconds, a straggler arrives that happened at 59.

33
00:02:12.590 --> 00:02:15.983
Its window is still open, until 60 plus 10.

34
00:02:15.983 --> 00:02:17.561
So it's counted: 61.

35
00:02:17.767 --> 00:02:19.730
At 70, the window is final.

36
00:02:19.730 --> 00:02:23.075
61 goes out, once, and the program forgets it.

37
00:02:23.075 --> 00:02:27.728
That wait is the price: every result comes out 10 seconds later.

38
00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:31.098
The second window closes at 130, with 60.

39
00:02:31.098 --> 00:02:34.800
Then, at 150, a payment from 100 seconds arrives.

40
00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:37.898
Its window is gone, and its count is out.

41
00:02:37.898 --> 00:02:41.979
So it's dropped as late, and the late counter goes up.

42
00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:43.956
Here's the lesson's test.

43
00:02:43.956 --> 00:02:48.801
Three stragglers arrive 5, 30 and 90 seconds after their windows end.

44
00:02:48.801 --> 00:02:51.751
With 10 seconds of grace, two are dropped.

45
00:02:51.751 --> 00:02:54.208
With 60 seconds of grace, how many?

46
00:02:54.208 --> 00:02:55.332
Pause and guess.

47
00:02:58.567 --> 00:02:58.830
One.

48
00:02:58.830 --> 00:03:00.743
Only the one 90 seconds late.

49
00:03:00.743 --> 00:03:05.227
But now every window waits a whole minute before its count goes out.

50
00:03:05.227 --> 00:03:10.305
More grace keeps more stragglers, and delays every result by the same amount.

51
00:03:10.667 --> 00:03:14.768
Remember, a watermark is a guess, and it can be wrong two ways.

52
00:03:14.768 --> 00:03:19.326
Too fast: an older event still arrives, from a phone that was offline.

53
00:03:19.326 --> 00:03:20.628
That's a late event.

54
00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:27.435
Too slow: Flink, another engine, reads several inputs and takes the smallest of their watermarks.

55
00:03:27.435 --> 00:03:30.129
One quiet input holds every window open.

56
00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:36.934
It's like refusing to add up July until every friend has sent a card, including the one who never writes.

57
00:03:36.934 --> 00:03:41.165
So you mark a quiet input as idle, and it stops holding things back.

58
00:03:41.533 --> 00:03:48.388
In Kafka Streams, in Java and Kotlin, it's one line: one-minute windows, with 10 seconds of grace.

59
00:03:49.333 --> 00:03:52.253
So count by the postmark, not by the letterbox.

60
00:03:52.253 --> 00:03:58.093
The watermark says when a count is final, and the grace says how long you wait for stragglers.

61
00:03:58.567 --> 00:03:59.938
One quick check.

62
00:03:59.938 --> 00:04:04.139
Grace is 10 seconds, and the watermark is at 125.

63
00:04:04.139 --> 00:04:07.140
A payment from 115 seconds arrives.

64
00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:08.855
Counted, or dropped?

65
00:04:08.855 --> 00:04:10.227
Pause and think.

66
00:04:13.433 --> 00:04:14.367
Counted.

67
00:04:14.367 --> 00:04:19.505
Its window, 60 to 120, stays open until 130.
